WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.458 --> 00:00:01.549
Faith.

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:08.742
This is the best, for
all the wrong reasons.

00:00:08.742 --> 00:00:09.720
[laughs]

00:00:09.720 --> 00:00:11.687
Hi, my name is Charlie Santore.

00:00:11.687 --> 00:00:14.400
I'm the owner of Santore
and Son Lock and Safe.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:16.940
I'm a safe technician,
meaning safecracker,

00:00:16.940 --> 00:00:18.390
in Southern California.

00:00:18.390 --> 00:00:20.198
Today we're going to look at some clips

00:00:20.198 --> 00:00:22.198
on safecracking in films,

00:00:22.198 --> 00:00:24.040
and we'll tell you
whether they're credible

00:00:24.040 --> 00:00:25.273
or whether they're garbage.

00:00:29.781 --> 00:00:31.800
[safe clicking]

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:35.060
The clicking dial that
Hollywood loves so much.

00:00:35.060 --> 00:00:38.080
They typically have a
bearing surface on it.

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:40.180
It's usually very, very smooth.

00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:42.130
If it was properly installed,

00:00:42.130 --> 00:00:43.220
it shouldn't be clicking.

00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:46.060
There's, like, one or two models

00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:49.300
of a junky safe that
you can get at Staples

00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:50.610
where the dial clicks.

00:00:50.610 --> 00:00:51.935
It's not really a real thing.

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:58.640
The sort of dialing sequence
that she would be doing

00:00:58.640 --> 00:01:01.986
in order to do so is not
how she's dialing here.

00:01:01.986 --> 00:01:05.827
She's actually working backwards

00:01:05.827 --> 00:01:08.110
to how we typically would.

00:01:08.110 --> 00:01:09.777
The principle of it is that

00:01:09.777 --> 00:01:12.760
wheels are being searched for low spots.

00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:15.980
And what that involves
is parking certain wheels

00:01:15.980 --> 00:01:17.350
and moving other wheels.

00:01:17.350 --> 00:01:19.960
And there's a certain repetitive sequence

00:01:19.960 --> 00:01:23.890
to doing that by people
that know how to do this.

00:01:23.890 --> 00:01:25.900
And she's not doing this here.

00:01:25.900 --> 00:01:28.141
Come on, Charlie.

00:01:28.141 --> 00:01:29.807
You can do this.

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:35.950
There's a difference between
people burglarizing things

00:01:35.950 --> 00:01:38.215
and people opening things

00:01:38.215 --> 00:01:40.800
the way that we open them
to put them back in service.

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:42.003
They're not going to be sitting around

00:01:42.003 --> 00:01:43.730
trying to manipulate a safe open.

00:01:43.730 --> 00:01:45.080
They would drill it open.

00:01:45.080 --> 00:01:47.410
Even opening a safe like this,

00:01:47.410 --> 00:01:48.870
even if you're drilling it,

00:01:48.870 --> 00:01:50.400
is also extremely difficult.

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:52.780
You're not haphazardly
drilling holes in something.

00:01:52.780 --> 00:01:54.380
You're not vandalizing something.

00:01:54.380 --> 00:01:55.580
When we open safes,

00:01:55.580 --> 00:01:58.960
the idea is it's like
arthroscopic surgery.

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:01.850
We're doing it in a
minimally invasive way.

00:02:01.850 --> 00:02:04.030
The whole thing is a very academic study.

00:02:04.030 --> 00:02:06.672
[clicking]

00:02:11.201 --> 00:02:12.034
[safe clicks open]

00:02:12.034 --> 00:02:15.533
We're supposed to believe
when that click happened,

00:02:15.533 --> 00:02:19.420
that final click is the
fence of the lock dropping.

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:22.330
But here, with vault locks and safe locks,

00:02:22.330 --> 00:02:24.030
you have to do a final turn

00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:25.840
to pull the lock bolt back.

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:27.610
There's a certain amount of travel

00:02:27.610 --> 00:02:29.190
that then opens that lock.

00:02:29.190 --> 00:02:31.878
So she dials the combination,

00:02:31.878 --> 00:02:35.200
here she stops at 50, the fence drops,

00:02:35.200 --> 00:02:37.500
she's saying the safe is open,

00:02:37.500 --> 00:02:38.580
and he turns the handle.

00:02:38.580 --> 00:02:40.040
But the safe wouldn't be open

00:02:40.040 --> 00:02:41.720
even if the lock was unlocked

00:02:41.720 --> 00:02:44.070
because she didn't retract the lock bolt.

00:02:44.070 --> 00:02:46.240
This notion of somebody creeping around

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:48.660
and manipulating a safe open,

00:02:48.660 --> 00:02:50.600
it does happen and it has happened,

00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:51.883
but nine times out of 10,

00:02:51.883 --> 00:02:53.674
it's sledgehammers and wedges

00:02:53.674 --> 00:02:56.410
or grinders or saws
cutting the thing open.

00:02:56.410 --> 00:02:58.635
So, I want to be generous. It's 3.

00:03:07.780 --> 00:03:10.490
He's taken the fingerprint
off the door with tape.

00:03:10.490 --> 00:03:12.220
It could have been the
cleaning lady's print

00:03:12.220 --> 00:03:13.745
that he pulled off of there.

00:03:18.620 --> 00:03:20.870
This actually broke my head a little bit.

00:03:20.870 --> 00:03:22.830
If you pull the print off with tape

00:03:22.830 --> 00:03:24.580
and you turn it over and put a ring on it

00:03:24.580 --> 00:03:25.610
and put Loctite in it,

00:03:25.610 --> 00:03:27.500
aren't you actually making it a cast

00:03:27.500 --> 00:03:29.400
of the inverse of the fingerprint?

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:31.310
This safe is serious.

00:03:31.310 --> 00:03:33.690
Luis: How serious are we talking, Scotty?

00:03:33.690 --> 00:03:35.070
It's a Carbondale.

00:03:35.070 --> 00:03:37.040
Carbondale's a made-up name,

00:03:37.040 --> 00:03:38.720
but the vault door itself,

00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:41.050
I looked through hundreds and hundreds

00:03:41.050 --> 00:03:43.320
of photos of vault doors,

00:03:43.320 --> 00:03:46.873
and I'm pretty certain
that the art directors

00:03:46.873 --> 00:03:50.982
based this off of a very
early Diebold vault door.

00:03:54.380 --> 00:03:57.310
This is actually a real tool.

00:03:57.310 --> 00:04:00.160
This is a StrongArm MiniRig

00:04:00.160 --> 00:04:02.710
that's used for putting pressure

00:04:02.710 --> 00:04:04.030
when one's drilling.

00:04:04.030 --> 00:04:07.697
So that aspect of it
is definitely accurate.

00:04:12.570 --> 00:04:15.330
We'll go do a quick job that
winds up being something

00:04:15.330 --> 00:04:17.860
where you don't have your
full tool kit with you.

00:04:17.860 --> 00:04:21.120
So there's lots of times when
we've been running around

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:23.970
trying to bend wires and make tools

00:04:23.970 --> 00:04:26.250
and sort of have to come up with something

00:04:26.250 --> 00:04:27.858
that we didn't have with us.

00:04:32.450 --> 00:04:34.400
Supposedly he's freezing it

00:04:34.400 --> 00:04:37.550
and it's going to expand
and crack this door apart.

00:04:37.550 --> 00:04:39.703
What they showed us was the bolt work

00:04:39.703 --> 00:04:42.260
inside of the safe and the door pan.

00:04:42.260 --> 00:04:43.740
There's a big door pan.

00:04:43.740 --> 00:04:45.500
If he poured a gallon of water in there,

00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:48.570
it would spill out the bottom
of the door pan, basically.

00:04:48.570 --> 00:04:51.220
If by chance he froze it

00:04:51.220 --> 00:04:52.870
and the ice expanded, I mean,

00:04:52.870 --> 00:04:55.610
obviously water can expand.

00:04:55.610 --> 00:04:58.560
Glaciers expand over time.

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:00.000
The thing about a vault door

00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:02.880
is, in this case,
there's a thin back panel

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:04.863
that's like sheet metal essentially

00:05:04.863 --> 00:05:06.421
on the back of the door.

00:05:06.421 --> 00:05:11.190
So the idea that that's going
to expand is pure fantasy.

00:05:11.190 --> 00:05:13.202
It would blow the back panel off

00:05:13.202 --> 00:05:14.488
before it blew the door off.

00:05:15.330 --> 00:05:16.383
There's nothing here.

00:05:16.383 --> 00:05:17.500
What'd you say?

00:05:18.680 --> 00:05:20.120
It's a suit.

00:05:20.120 --> 00:05:21.600
I opened up a couple of safes

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:24.580
that were at a Blockbuster Video a while

00:05:24.580 --> 00:05:28.240
and there was Astroglide,
condoms, and some dirty photos.

00:05:28.240 --> 00:05:30.224
So I don't know if a manager

00:05:30.224 --> 00:05:32.550
was having a fling with somebody,

00:05:32.550 --> 00:05:34.660
but that was pretty unexpected.

00:05:34.660 --> 00:05:37.100
On the more valuable side of things,

00:05:37.100 --> 00:05:38.883
we once opened a safe

00:05:38.883 --> 00:05:40.983
in an affluent part of Beverly Hills

00:05:40.983 --> 00:05:43.160
that people had recently
moved into a home.

00:05:43.160 --> 00:05:45.370
The former owner was gone.

00:05:45.370 --> 00:05:48.767
There were Bulgari
necklaces, Cartier necklaces,

00:05:48.767 --> 00:05:51.250
Krugerrands, GIA certificates.

00:05:51.250 --> 00:05:53.820
I mean, I can only imagine that it

00:05:53.820 --> 00:05:57.760
must have been a mid-
to high-six-figure lick.

00:05:57.760 --> 00:05:59.570
But I think that people get traumatized

00:05:59.570 --> 00:06:00.900
when there's that much left

00:06:00.900 --> 00:06:02.450
because they're not expecting

00:06:02.450 --> 00:06:04.739
to have a moral conflict, sort of.

00:06:04.739 --> 00:06:07.088
You know, "It's technically mine,

00:06:07.088 --> 00:06:09.096
but this obviously meant a great deal

00:06:09.096 --> 00:06:12.190
to whoever it was that
was living here before."

00:06:12.190 --> 00:06:15.030
It's nonsense, but the MiniRig is real.

00:06:15.030 --> 00:06:17.300
So I'm going to give it a 3.

00:06:17.300 --> 00:06:20.470
Scott: How would you
like to make 250 grand

00:06:20.470 --> 00:06:22.840
for one day's work?

00:06:22.840 --> 00:06:24.350
Oh, my God.

00:06:24.350 --> 00:06:27.070
There are certainly a lot of safes

00:06:27.070 --> 00:06:29.520
that are known among people

00:06:29.520 --> 00:06:30.500
who do this kind of work

00:06:30.500 --> 00:06:32.540
that are very, very difficult to open.

00:06:32.540 --> 00:06:34.457
Very well-guarded locks.

00:06:34.457 --> 00:06:37.407
So, yeah, there is some truth to that.

00:06:37.407 --> 00:06:39.720
But of the world's living locksmiths,

00:06:39.720 --> 00:06:42.860
do I represent your greatest
chance of opening it?

00:06:42.860 --> 00:06:44.652
I can, with humility,

00:06:44.652 --> 00:06:45.752
say yes.

00:06:45.752 --> 00:06:49.360
The biggest myth I think
is that there's some boffin

00:06:49.360 --> 00:06:53.983
or there's some maven
who has the, you know,

00:06:53.983 --> 00:06:56.340
magic touch to get the thing open.

00:06:56.340 --> 00:06:57.851
The four locks?

00:06:57.851 --> 00:06:59.767
You know what that is?

00:06:59.767 --> 00:07:01.910
Scott: No.
Dieter: No, of course not.

00:07:01.910 --> 00:07:04.530
It's a rotating randomization mechanism.

00:07:04.530 --> 00:07:06.800
If you mess it up again, it locks forever.

00:07:06.800 --> 00:07:08.660
You understand? Forever.

00:07:08.660 --> 00:07:11.160
Las Vegas is overrun with zombies.

00:07:11.160 --> 00:07:13.440
The idea that they're
going to bring some genius

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:15.804
to come and manipulate open four locks

00:07:15.804 --> 00:07:18.210
on a vault door is ridiculous.

00:07:18.210 --> 00:07:19.950
They would just bring a coring crew.

00:07:19.950 --> 00:07:21.940
They'd bring coring stuff,

00:07:21.940 --> 00:07:23.990
and they're bore a hole
in the side of the vault

00:07:23.990 --> 00:07:25.010
and get it open.

00:07:25.010 --> 00:07:28.620
How long will it take you to open it?

00:07:28.620 --> 00:07:29.750
30 minutes.

00:07:29.750 --> 00:07:32.225
The idea that somebody
would manipulate open

00:07:32.225 --> 00:07:36.110
four vault locks in half
an hour is pure fantasy.

00:07:36.110 --> 00:07:38.777
[lock clicking]

00:07:40.490 --> 00:07:42.700
He's supposedly manipulating this open.

00:07:42.700 --> 00:07:44.530
He's not even looking at the dial,

00:07:44.530 --> 00:07:47.870
which is where he would be
looking for the indications

00:07:47.870 --> 00:07:49.970
that he would use to manipulate it open.

00:07:49.970 --> 00:07:52.150
And there's also some hieroglyphs

00:07:52.150 --> 00:07:54.570
or some scrawling that he wrote on

00:07:54.570 --> 00:07:56.280
or he drew on the front of the door

00:07:56.280 --> 00:07:58.185
that really has nothing to do with

00:07:58.185 --> 00:08:00.276
what he would be doing in
order to get this open.

00:08:04.920 --> 00:08:06.470
The doors are always heavy.

00:08:06.470 --> 00:08:09.410
You're talking about a
multi-multi-ton door.

00:08:09.410 --> 00:08:11.669
There are compression systems

00:08:11.669 --> 00:08:13.810
that'd have spinning wheels.

00:08:13.810 --> 00:08:15.520
Not all vault doors have those.

00:08:15.520 --> 00:08:16.787
Some have handles.

00:08:18.980 --> 00:08:21.650
According to him, this
vault has four locks on it.

00:08:21.650 --> 00:08:23.510
When the vault door swings open,

00:08:23.510 --> 00:08:24.690
we see that there's actually

00:08:24.690 --> 00:08:27.205
only two vault locks
on the back of the door

00:08:27.205 --> 00:08:29.490
and a time lock with five movements.

00:08:29.490 --> 00:08:31.810
I've never seen a five-movement time lock.

00:08:31.810 --> 00:08:35.318
I'm going to give it a 2
as far as the realism goes.

00:08:41.290 --> 00:08:43.560
He's using an autodialer.

00:08:43.560 --> 00:08:44.870
There are such tools.

00:08:44.870 --> 00:08:46.042
I mean, they don't look like

00:08:46.042 --> 00:08:48.900
a vacuum cleaner hooked
up to a Xerox machine.

00:08:48.900 --> 00:08:50.330
It's smaller, it's portable.

00:08:50.330 --> 00:08:53.712
You hook it up and it runs through,

00:08:53.712 --> 00:08:56.989
it parks wheels, and it runs
through a dialing sequence.

00:08:56.989 --> 00:09:00.100
The advantage is on some safes

00:09:00.100 --> 00:09:02.320
that have glass relockers in them

00:09:02.320 --> 00:09:04.613
or that might be problematic to drill

00:09:04.613 --> 00:09:07.610
or some people don't
have the necessary tools

00:09:07.610 --> 00:09:09.343
to open some of those safes.

00:09:15.120 --> 00:09:17.190
He's reading the paper at one point

00:09:17.190 --> 00:09:18.081
and he's sitting around.

00:09:18.081 --> 00:09:20.150
And that's, I mean,
that's pretty accurate.

00:09:20.150 --> 00:09:22.030
It's not an instantaneous process.

00:09:22.030 --> 00:09:25.340
The much earlier versions
sometimes could take anywhere

00:09:25.340 --> 00:09:29.170
from, like, I think 10 to
30 hours to open a safe.

00:09:29.170 --> 00:09:31.770
The newer ones are faster,

00:09:31.770 --> 00:09:33.990
and if one of the numbers is known,

00:09:33.990 --> 00:09:36.030
then it's even infinitely faster.

00:09:36.030 --> 00:09:38.120
A lot of times people set them up

00:09:38.120 --> 00:09:41.190
in their shop and they go off for the day

00:09:41.190 --> 00:09:42.700
and they come back the following day.

00:09:42.700 --> 00:09:44.790
We don't use an autodialer.

00:09:44.790 --> 00:09:47.740
People who are able to manipulate well

00:09:47.740 --> 00:09:51.157
can usually open something in

00:09:51.157 --> 00:09:53.298
30 minutes, 45 minutes.

00:09:56.700 --> 00:09:58.830
The weird and wonderful thing now

00:09:58.830 --> 00:10:00.180
is a tape measurer.

00:10:00.180 --> 00:10:02.340
A tape measurer is very, very useful.

00:10:02.340 --> 00:10:03.623
You know, they're telling kids that

00:10:03.623 --> 00:10:04.798
they don't have to learn math

00:10:04.798 --> 00:10:06.670
and that there's no right answers to math.

00:10:06.670 --> 00:10:09.030
You can't do this job without being able

00:10:09.030 --> 00:10:10.870
to read a tape measurer, that's for sure.

00:10:10.870 --> 00:10:13.290
But no, as far as high-tech tools go,

00:10:13.290 --> 00:10:17.340
there's lots of different
picks for certain key locks.

00:10:20.650 --> 00:10:23.465
People have to think of their safes

00:10:23.465 --> 00:10:26.700
and their vault doors as
the last line of defense.

00:10:26.700 --> 00:10:29.220
You should have alarms,
you should have cameras.

00:10:29.220 --> 00:10:32.660
Make sure there's no outlets
around in close proximity.

00:10:32.660 --> 00:10:33.920
If it's a smaller safe,

00:10:33.920 --> 00:10:36.460
if you can stick it in a horrible position

00:10:36.460 --> 00:10:38.150
where it's hard to get to,

00:10:38.150 --> 00:10:40.970
sometimes under cabinetry,
under furniture.

00:10:40.970 --> 00:10:43.410
And, truthfully, the best safe is one

00:10:43.410 --> 00:10:45.360
that no one knows is even there.

00:10:45.360 --> 00:10:48.010
So that's why in-floor
safes are very popular.

00:10:48.010 --> 00:10:50.500
Wall safe, someone can just
cut it out of the studs

00:10:50.500 --> 00:10:51.860
if they know that it's there.

00:10:51.860 --> 00:10:55.070
About a 4 or 5, 'cause
there are autodialers.

00:10:55.070 --> 00:10:57.107
Guard: My shoes are melting!

00:10:57.107 --> 00:10:58.221
Aah!

00:11:02.020 --> 00:11:05.360
The one element of this that is correct

00:11:05.360 --> 00:11:07.330
is that oftentimes

00:11:07.330 --> 00:11:09.250
when there's a vault lockout,

00:11:09.250 --> 00:11:12.460
a lot of vaults can be
opened from the inside,

00:11:12.460 --> 00:11:15.500
and there's a way to
open the door sometimes.

00:11:15.500 --> 00:11:18.610
But there's not another lock on the inside

00:11:18.610 --> 00:11:20.618
that somebody would have to crack.

00:11:22.496 --> 00:11:23.793
Hey!

00:11:23.793 --> 00:11:25.950
Hey, that's my hearing aid!

00:11:25.950 --> 00:11:27.850
As far as a listening device goes,

00:11:27.850 --> 00:11:29.460
people do use amps.

00:11:29.460 --> 00:11:32.925
It usually has a little
magnet on it, so it sticks.

00:11:32.925 --> 00:11:35.640
Or if it's a stainless thing
that it won't stick to,

00:11:35.640 --> 00:11:38.280
you can hold it there, tape it in place,

00:11:38.280 --> 00:11:39.830
and it's got a little pot on it

00:11:39.830 --> 00:11:41.310
that the level turns up.

00:11:41.310 --> 00:11:43.620
And when you're dealing with a metal door,

00:11:43.620 --> 00:11:47.050
the sound actually
travels very, very well.

00:11:47.050 --> 00:11:49.616
Just to be generous, we'll say 3.

00:11:52.670 --> 00:11:56.490
The main issue is if he
was using a cutting torch

00:11:56.490 --> 00:11:57.848
to cut that square,

00:11:57.848 --> 00:11:59.550
the gear that's right in back of there

00:11:59.550 --> 00:12:00.940
that supposedly he's moving

00:12:00.940 --> 00:12:02.540
would probably have been damaged,

00:12:02.540 --> 00:12:05.760
as well as any sort of
electrical components.

00:12:05.760 --> 00:12:08.430
There's lots of metal that comes off.

00:12:08.430 --> 00:12:10.280
As far as burglary goes,

00:12:10.280 --> 00:12:11.870
people use torches often.

00:12:11.870 --> 00:12:13.936
And even earlier on,

00:12:13.936 --> 00:12:18.752
safe technicians used to
use torches and lances

00:12:18.752 --> 00:12:20.700
because there's certain metals

00:12:20.700 --> 00:12:23.000
that are very, very hard to drill.

00:12:27.989 --> 00:12:30.740
Typically, vault locks
are a little bigger.

00:12:30.740 --> 00:12:32.820
Safe locks are a little smaller,

00:12:32.820 --> 00:12:35.880
but they all work on the same principle,

00:12:35.880 --> 00:12:38.289
that there's a blocking point.

00:12:38.289 --> 00:12:42.300
And so that aspect of
it, that something small

00:12:42.300 --> 00:12:44.940
is actually keeping
something large locked,

00:12:44.940 --> 00:12:46.820
that aspect of it is very true.

00:12:46.820 --> 00:12:49.260
A lot of vault doors use yokes.

00:12:49.260 --> 00:12:51.410
There's pivoting yokes,

00:12:51.410 --> 00:12:55.680
but it's still a lock
bolt attacked to a lock.

00:12:55.680 --> 00:12:58.450
I would say, like, a 3.5.

00:12:58.450 --> 00:13:01.820
I will say that there are certain locks

00:13:01.820 --> 00:13:04.650
that do have a stepper motor in them

00:13:04.650 --> 00:13:08.626
that, to open them sometimes
when they're being drilled,

00:13:08.626 --> 00:13:11.601
there's a gear that gets
turned a certain way.

00:13:15.180 --> 00:13:17.810
The only real issue that I probably have

00:13:17.810 --> 00:13:20.529
is they're using pneumatic drills.

00:13:20.529 --> 00:13:25.029
I doubt somebody would
use pneumatic lines.

00:13:29.680 --> 00:13:33.060
This comes closest to what we do

00:13:33.060 --> 00:13:34.510
on a regular basis.

00:13:39.080 --> 00:13:42.490
They've drilled into the
safe before they hit glass.

00:13:42.490 --> 00:13:46.500
Normally the glass would
be much, much closer

00:13:46.500 --> 00:13:48.110
to the back of the door,

00:13:48.110 --> 00:13:51.560
so you wouldn't have this
much working space in there.

00:13:51.560 --> 00:13:54.826
There's 2, 2 1/2, 3
inches of working space.

00:13:58.090 --> 00:14:00.270
You see them stick a borescope up.

00:14:00.270 --> 00:14:02.060
They find the cable loop.

00:14:02.060 --> 00:14:03.890
She sticks a wire in.

00:14:03.890 --> 00:14:06.170
She lassos the cable.

00:14:06.170 --> 00:14:07.850
The only real issue

00:14:07.850 --> 00:14:10.180
is that they're going to
tie this off to a chair

00:14:10.180 --> 00:14:11.365
with people sitting on it.

00:14:11.365 --> 00:14:13.640
That's nonsense. That's not necessary.

00:14:13.640 --> 00:14:16.380
All they'd have to do is
take vice grips probably

00:14:16.380 --> 00:14:18.240
and clamp them right at the door,

00:14:18.240 --> 00:14:21.630
and that cable isn't going anywhere.

00:14:21.630 --> 00:14:23.330
It's a spring-loaded thing.

00:14:23.330 --> 00:14:26.020
We have to assume that
she had intimate knowledge

00:14:26.020 --> 00:14:28.140
of what kind of safe it was.

00:14:28.140 --> 00:14:30.150
Somebody leaked the information to her.

00:14:30.150 --> 00:14:31.846
Nine times out of 10,

00:14:31.846 --> 00:14:34.275
somebody knows that there
was a safe somewhere,

00:14:34.275 --> 00:14:36.330
and it's not necessarily the person

00:14:36.330 --> 00:14:38.630
who knew that it was
there, but a lot of times,

00:14:38.630 --> 00:14:40.783
they sell the information
to somebody else,

00:14:40.783 --> 00:14:42.240
they pass it on to somebody else,

00:14:42.240 --> 00:14:44.930
like Paul Rudd coming into your house

00:14:44.930 --> 00:14:47.210
with an air mattress and Freon

00:14:47.210 --> 00:14:50.010
and a jug of water to
blow your vault door off.

00:14:50.010 --> 00:14:53.730
It's, like, probably a 9 or a 9.5

00:14:53.730 --> 00:14:54.950
as far as that goes.

00:14:54.950 --> 00:14:56.984
It's one of the best
that we've seen so far.

00:15:05.410 --> 00:15:08.418
This is the heist that happened

00:15:08.418 --> 00:15:11.543
at 88-90 Hatton Garden,

00:15:11.543 --> 00:15:15.152
where a bunch of geriatric ex-gangsters

00:15:15.152 --> 00:15:17.930
cored the vault, and they breached it,

00:15:17.930 --> 00:15:20.560
and they cleaned out
I think 70-plus boxes.

00:15:20.560 --> 00:15:23.293
When this story broke,
I was obsessed with it.

00:15:27.500 --> 00:15:29.850
I think they're even using the exact gear

00:15:29.850 --> 00:15:34.104
that was actually used
to get into the vault.

00:15:34.104 --> 00:15:35.519
And my understanding is

00:15:35.519 --> 00:15:37.723
it happened over a three-day weekend.

00:15:38.650 --> 00:15:42.477
They wound up using a coring rig.

00:15:42.477 --> 00:15:44.530
They cored overlapping holes.

00:15:44.530 --> 00:15:47.520
When they realized that they
had to get a pneumatic pump,

00:15:47.520 --> 00:15:51.650
they built a brace and they
put a pneumatic pump in

00:15:51.650 --> 00:15:54.925
in order to force the
boxes away from the wall.

00:15:59.305 --> 00:16:02.175
The boxes obviously inside of this vault

00:16:02.175 --> 00:16:03.490
are not the same boxes

00:16:03.490 --> 00:16:06.010
that were inside the
actual Hatton Garden vault,

00:16:06.010 --> 00:16:08.150
because the photos that we've seen

00:16:08.150 --> 00:16:11.130
was a hodgepodge of different
kind of locks and boxes,

00:16:11.130 --> 00:16:12.630
different-sized boxes.

00:16:12.630 --> 00:16:14.600
In this one, they're much more uniform.

00:16:14.600 --> 00:16:16.869
This really, I mean, is, like, probably,

00:16:16.869 --> 00:16:20.859
like, a 9.5 or a 10 out of 10, really.

00:16:20.859 --> 00:16:21.984
Faith.

00:16:26.753 --> 00:16:29.390
This is the best, for
all the wrong reasons.

00:16:29.390 --> 00:16:30.223
[laughs]

00:16:30.223 --> 00:16:32.320
DMX, rest in peace,

00:16:32.320 --> 00:16:36.103
but all I'll say is that

00:16:36.103 --> 00:16:38.044
if DMX shows up at your house

00:16:38.044 --> 00:16:40.330
with a bazooka to open your vault,

00:16:40.330 --> 00:16:41.950
your problem's getting out of hand.

00:16:41.950 --> 00:16:43.930
It's so entertainingly stupid.

00:16:43.930 --> 00:16:45.240
We have to look at this.

00:16:45.240 --> 00:16:48.210
We're on the fourth
floor of a bank building.

00:16:48.210 --> 00:16:50.238
They're shooting a bazooka through a wall.

00:16:50.238 --> 00:16:51.071
I mean, it's,

00:16:51.071 --> 00:16:52.050
we're going to give it a 3.

00:16:52.050 --> 00:16:54.833
Not based on anything technical at all.

00:17:01.100 --> 00:17:04.470
The tool that James Caan is using is real.

00:17:04.470 --> 00:17:08.240
The drill bit that James
Caan is using in there

00:17:08.240 --> 00:17:11.000
is about this big, OK?

00:17:11.000 --> 00:17:13.900
And the kind of bit that we typically use

00:17:13.900 --> 00:17:17.130
to drill open a safe if
we're scoping something

00:17:17.130 --> 00:17:18.734
is about this big.

00:17:23.440 --> 00:17:25.216
But if you look at the proximity

00:17:25.216 --> 00:17:28.860
of how the wheels sit
to where that dial is

00:17:28.860 --> 00:17:31.120
and the way the light is
shining on the wheels,

00:17:31.120 --> 00:17:34.070
my sense is that this is
a little bit of a mock-up.

00:17:34.070 --> 00:17:37.618
There are many, many safes, older safes

00:17:37.618 --> 00:17:42.584
that utilize a punching
technique like that.

00:17:42.584 --> 00:17:44.520
In this situation, I don't believe

00:17:44.520 --> 00:17:47.220
that that's what would
have been necessary.

00:17:47.220 --> 00:17:50.010
I mean, especially with the hole that big.

00:17:50.010 --> 00:17:51.880
The gates could have been dialed up.

00:17:51.880 --> 00:17:55.002
But, again, I think that
we're coming up against

00:17:55.002 --> 00:17:57.540
trying to have authentic realism

00:17:57.540 --> 00:18:01.580
and trying to maintain a
certain amount of secrecy

00:18:01.580 --> 00:18:03.770
and trying to give the
audience a little taste

00:18:03.770 --> 00:18:05.540
of what's actually real.

00:18:05.540 --> 00:18:09.790
I would have to give this,
like, a 8.5, probably.

00:18:09.790 --> 00:18:14.560
On a slow year, we open
probably 300-plus safes.

00:18:14.560 --> 00:18:16.960
On a busy year, probably closer to 500.

00:18:16.960 --> 00:18:20.610
I get to maybe exercise
that part of my shadow,

00:18:20.610 --> 00:18:23.510
and I get to help people in the process.

00:18:23.510 --> 00:18:26.170
So, I mean, I can't think
of a thing I'd rather do.

00:18:26.170 --> 00:18:27.820
Thank you very much for watching.

00:18:27.820 --> 00:18:30.937
If you liked what you saw,
click on the link above.

